  "raw_text": "Step 1: To find the integer \\( n \\) that satisfies \\( 0 \\leq n < 18 \\) and \\( n \\equiv -11213141 \\pmod{18} \\), we first need to simplify \\(-11213141\\) modulo 18.\n\nStep 2: We can simplify \\(-11213141\\) by finding its remainder when divided by 18. This is equivalent to calculating \\(-11213141 \\mod 18\\).\n\nStep 3: To do this, we can break down the number into smaller parts for easier calculation. Notice that \\(11213141 = 11213140 + 1 = 622657 \\times 18 + 14 + 1 = 622657 \\times 18 + 15\\).\n\nStep 4: Therefore, \\(-11213141 = -(622657 \\times 18 + 15) = -622657 \\times 18 - 15\\).\n\nStep 5: When taking modulo 18, \\(-622657 \\times 18 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{18}\\), so we have \\(-11213141 \\equiv -15 \\pmod{18}\\).\n\nStep 6: Since \\(-15\\) is negative, we convert it to a positive equivalent by adding 18: \\(-15 + 18 = 3\\).\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{3}<|im_end|>",
